WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.400
- When is dancing a protest?

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.879
- When it's Bomba.

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:07.250
(drums playing)

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.350
- Bomba is a traditional
genre of music and dance

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:11.460
from Puerto Rico

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.580
and has made an appearance

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.293
at many recent Black
Lives Matter protests.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.400
- In Bomba, dancing and protests
are inextricably linked.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.700
Surprised?

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.186
Once you understand the
music, it makes perfect sense.

00:00:24.186 --> 00:00:26.769
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.200
- So now that we know
Bomba's influence today,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.080
let's talk about where it came from.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.090
And to do that,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.463
we have to go back about 400 years.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.570
Last year our friends from,
If Cities Could Dance,

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.010
traveled to Puerto Rico

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.180
and filmed this footage for
their episode on Bomba dancing.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.216
Make sure you check out that
episode on their channel.

00:00:46.216 --> 00:00:49.310
(drums playing)

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.300
- Bomba was developed in the 16th century

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.470
by enslaved Africans in Puerto Rico

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.730
enslaved communities would gather

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.940
to make music using rhythms and
dance to express themselves.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.700
They told stories, shared
news, communicated revolts

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.280
and connected with each other.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.030
This process of gathering through Bomba

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.080
was known as Bambula, a
practice of re- remembrance.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.610
Re-remembering where you came from

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.290
and your own sense of humanity.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.620
In other words,

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.280
they created culture and
affirmed their humanity

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.460
and that is a powerful form of resistance.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.670
How would you describe Bomba

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.210
to people who haven't heard it before?

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.110
or who haven't heard of it even?

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.440
- Well Bomba is the oldest
genre of Puerto Rico

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.670
tracing back to our African roots,

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.430
it tells the story of our ancestors.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.280
It is meant for healing.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.330
It is a time-traveling genre

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.540
and it's Black music.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:36.960
- Yes.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.820
Bomb's roots largely come from Africa

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.680
but like so much of Latinx culture,

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.320
it's a blending of
elements from many regions.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.410
While most Bomba songs are in Spanish.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.530
Some have words and
phrases in other languages

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.846
including Haitian Creole and Kikongo.

00:01:49.846 --> 00:01:51.660
(drums playing)

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.180
The use of long ruffled
skirts as Bomba costumes

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.480
also shows incorporation
of Spanish influences

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.624
in Bomba today.

00:01:57.624 --> 00:02:00.291
(drums playing)

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.900
The Taino where the
indigenous people of Boriken,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.060
their name for the Island
that is now Puerto Rico.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.900
That's also the reason many Puerto Rican's

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.790
prefer the term Boricua.

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:10.930
Taino influence in Bomba

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.480
shows up in terms like, Batey,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.231
which refers to the dance space.

00:02:14.231 --> 00:02:16.898
(drums playing)

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.140
- The use of a single gourd based maraca

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.550
also shows Taino influence.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.710
The maraca is used to keep time

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.036
and it's often played by the singer.

00:02:27.036 --> 00:02:30.840
Cuas are sticks, often played
against the side of a Barril

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.040
or the drum used for Bomba.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.220
Can you talk about the significance

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.150
of the Barril or the drum in Bomba

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:40.460
and like why this specific drum is used?

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.687
- The Barril, I would say in Spanish

00:02:42.687 --> 00:02:44.241
but in English, its barrel.

00:02:44.241 --> 00:02:45.074
- [LA Buckner] Barrel.

00:02:45.074 --> 00:02:46.230
- It's made out of a barrel.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.140
But its made out of,

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.900
a rum barrel, tea barrel, whiskey barrels.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.930
Like whatever type of
barrel you can think of,

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.260
it's made from that.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.510
That's what we had when

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.323
those ensalved Africans came..
- Ancestors.

00:02:57.323 --> 00:02:59.680
- Came from the motherland
into the Caribbean.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.520
- Yeah.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.110
- And then they needed their resource

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.760
and because they had all these rhythms

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.290
so they used the resources that they had

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:09.585
and what we use on, when
it comes to the skin,

00:03:09.585 --> 00:03:10.947
it's goat skin.

00:03:10.947 --> 00:03:11.780
- [LA Buckner] Yes.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.280
- So now we're talking
about a type of drum

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.665
that relates to the Djembe family.

00:03:16.665 --> 00:03:17.498
- Yes.

00:03:17.498 --> 00:03:19.688
- [Mateo Gonzalez] Well,
all those African based..

00:03:19.688 --> 00:03:21.160
- Absolutely
- [Mateo Gonzalez] Drums.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.960
- So what makes the
rhythms of Bomba, Bomba?

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.670
First, there are two types of Bomba drums.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.010
The lead drummer is known
as the Subidor or Primo

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.150
and plays a higher pitched Barril.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.260
The other drummers play a Buleador

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.856
and keep a steady rhythm called a buleo.

00:03:37.856 --> 00:03:40.523
(drums playing)

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.540
While that steady rhythm is going,

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.680
a dancer enters the
Batey, by doing a Paseo,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.410
walking around in a circle
to mark their space,

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.590
ending with a salute to the Primo.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.670
One of the most surprising
things about Bomba

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:58.940
is that the lead drummers
job, is actually to follow.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.890
As the dancer begins to dance,

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:03.310
the Primo follows their
movements with the drum,

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.980
striking to the rhythm
of the dancers steps.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.430
It's a conversation.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:10.350
Dancers use common dance
moves and improvisations

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.290
to express their thoughts and emotions.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.410
The drummer watches closely,

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.500
anticipating, following and giving voice

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.953
to their movements with the drum.

00:04:19.953 --> 00:04:22.620
(drums playing)

00:04:24.284 --> 00:04:26.400
For the enslaved people
who created the music form,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.440
Bomba was one of the
only vehicles they had

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.660
to express themselves
freely and to feel powerful.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.700
Reclaiming their sense of
humanity and self determination

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.223
was an act of resistance.

00:04:36.223 --> 00:04:39.500
(singing in foreign language)

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.250
Bomba has a collection
of rhythmic patterns

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.910
each used for different
purposes or emotions.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:45.980
For example,

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.778
Sica, means the act of rising up.

00:04:48.778 --> 00:04:51.445
(drums playing)

00:04:55.654 --> 00:04:57.113
Cuembe is to restore balance.

00:04:57.113 --> 00:04:59.780
(drums playing)

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.900
Seis Corrido is a faster rhythm,

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:09.653
traditionally played in Loiza.

00:05:09.653 --> 00:05:12.320
(drums playing)

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.380
Yuba is good for releasing
negative emotions

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.540
like sadness and anger.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.195
It refers to an ancestral ritual

00:05:22.195 --> 00:05:24.580
of cleansing space and self.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.480
You can feel that in the heaviness

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.144
of the last beat in the pattern.

00:05:28.144 --> 00:05:30.811
(drums playing)

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.090
I know Sica, Cuembe,
Seis Corrido and Yuba.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.401
What was the other one you said?

00:05:42.401 --> 00:05:43.370
- Holande.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.650
Just hearing it, holande.
- Holande.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:48.450
- Is influenced by the Dutch.
- Okay.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:50.110
- The way we dance Holande

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.523
is mimicking the slave owners,

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:54.631
how they dance.

00:05:54.631 --> 00:05:55.464
- Wow!

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.823
- So how we dance Holande
is influenced by them.

00:05:59.823 --> 00:06:02.490
(drums playing)

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:11.240
- Bomba lyrics are simple
and reflect daily life.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.670
Singers also take influence

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:13.900
from the drumming and the dancing

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:15.320
that they see in front of them.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:16.830
Generally, the lead singer

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.920
will start singing a short chorus.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.560
The chorus is repeated by others

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.289
as lead singer adds
improvised versus in between.

00:06:23.289 --> 00:06:27.122
(singing in foreign language)

00:06:28.069 --> 00:06:32.250
The chorus the people,
the community responds.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:36.083
(singing in foreign language)

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.771
And then I start storytelling.

00:06:39.771 --> 00:06:43.604
(singing in foreign language)

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.030
And so I start talking.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:52.650
So there are some songs
that are written, right?

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:56.240
But there's also this beautiful
aspect of singing Bomba

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.060
where you are just in the zone.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:59.410
You're there.
- Mhm.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:03.410
- We got so many beautiful
Bomba songs everywhere

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.270
that are traditional, that are written.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:07.940
We also have space to improvise.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.320
- That call response format

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:12.520
is another example of African
influence in Bomba music.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:13.930
It's also one reason

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.320
that there's always a place
for everyone at a Bombazo

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.461
and audience members
are often participants.

00:07:18.461 --> 00:07:20.610
(drums playing)

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.300
Throughout the Puerto Rican diaspora,

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.800
there are families, schools,
and performance groups

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:25.900
keeping Bomba alive

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.210
and passing the art form
to new generations today.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.900
Today, people who practice
Bomba continue to use it

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:33.750
as a platform for awareness,
resistance and pride.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.380
Bomba is much more than a dance or music.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:37.610
It's a way of life.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:41.020
- Talk about the
significance of Bomba to you.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.855
- Bomba is life.
- Mhm.

00:07:42.855 --> 00:07:45.412
- Because especially I was
raised in this tradition.

00:07:45.412 --> 00:07:47.940
You know, I was raised to just one person.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:51.273
Definitely influenced by my grandfather.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:54.562
But everybody knows him as, Benny.

00:07:54.562 --> 00:07:58.964
Unfortunately, I just lost
him a week and a half ago.

00:07:58.964 --> 00:08:00.868
- Oh, I'm sorry to hear that bro.

00:08:00.868 --> 00:08:02.128
- Yeah, thank you.

00:08:02.128 --> 00:08:04.037
- And the Cepeda Family of of San Juan,

00:08:04.037 --> 00:08:06.790
one of Puerto Rico's most
famous Bomba families.

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:09.290
The music has been passed
down for eight generations

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.320
and the Cepeda's are known

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.366
for having created the first Bomba school.

00:08:12.366 --> 00:08:17.040
(drums playing)
(singing in foreign language)

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:17.980
In recent years,

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:19.720
Bomba has been a balm for those suffering

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.170
from the aftermath of Hurricane Maria.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.250
After the storm, people
gathered to dance Bomba

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.720
as a way to express their
feelings of pain and loss

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.128
and come together as a community.

00:08:28.128 --> 00:08:30.795
(drums playing)

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:34.050
And now Bomba is an integral part

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:35.330
of Black Lives Matter movements

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:36.907
throughout the Puerto Rican diaspora.

00:08:36.907 --> 00:08:39.574
(drums playing)

00:08:40.728 --> 00:08:44.070
- Loiza is a center of
Afro-Boricua culture on the Island.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.930
After the murder of George Floyd,

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.360
Loiza was one of the first
Latin American communities

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.370
to organize demonstrations.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:54.640
Bomba and Plena were central
to their vigils and protests.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.790
(drums playing)

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.490
- Why do you think Bomba

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.900
has been so present in Black
Lives Matter movements lately?

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:03.540
Is it that authenticity?

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:04.500
That community?

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.050
I feel like there's so much.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:07.280
- The reason why we're out here

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.210
protesting of Black Lives Matter

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.120
is because we are reclaiming our spaces.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:13.430
You know, when we step back,

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:14.800
when we say black

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.970
there's our Afro Latinos, Afro Latinas.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:19.860
There's so much to what Black means.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.160
This is the music that
existed when we were oppressed

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:24.730
and we're still oppressed.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:29.170
So this is a way that we
need to stop that oppression

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:32.340
and to bring new ways of thinking.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:33.173
- Mhm.

00:09:33.173 --> 00:09:37.020
- We're authentically
us, beautiful in our skin

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:38.750
no matter the color of our skin.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:40.200
But when we bring Bomba,

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.010
we're talking about Black roots.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:45.150
- Whether traditional or
fused with other styles,

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.448
Bomba is a reclaiming of
Puerto Rican's African heritage

00:09:48.448 --> 00:09:50.740
and a way of countering racism

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.480
and other injustices in their
communities and in the world.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:58.040
What aspect of Bomba do you
find the most interesting

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:02.250
or what aspect of Bomba music
do you respect the most?

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.010
- I feel like I came very
much into my black identity

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.830
later than most of my peers,

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:08.360
especially being Puerto Rican

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:09.450
and you know, Bomba's Puerto Rican,

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.090
but there's a lot of
anti-blackness everywhere.

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:13.710
Especially though in Latino cultures

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.520
and I feel like people
don't think about it.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.160
But you know, when you think Puerto Rican,

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.400
you think J.Lo,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.640
you think Marc Anthony,

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.700
you think like, you know..
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:22.740
- First of all, I'm black,

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:23.573
look at me in black,

00:10:23.573 --> 00:10:25.190
but also (laughing loudly)

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:28.380
like this is my culture and
I found it through music.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:31.040
And so it's like an archive, you know?

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:31.873
- Yeah, yeah.

00:10:31.873 --> 00:10:32.990
Absolutely.
- And I think it's so cool.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:35.110
It felt kinda like when you
do one of those ancestry tests

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:36.590
and you're like, wow, you know,

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:37.960
it felt kinda like that for me.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:40.110
It's just so illuminating

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:42.570
that there are so many people
that share these stories

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:43.570
and share these histories.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:44.403
And like, you know,

00:10:44.403 --> 00:10:47.690
that is part of what has
made me the person that I am.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.350
And that's like my,

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:50.414
really my history.

00:10:50.414 --> 00:10:54.490
(drums playing)
(signing in foreign language)

